var definitions = ['<div>The French word <b>le garçon</b> translates to <b>the boy</b> in English. It refers to a young male child or adolescent.</div><div><b>Le garçon</b> mange une pomme.<br><b>The boy</b> is eating an apple.</div><div><b>Le garçon</b> joue au football.<br><b>The boy</b> is playing soccer.</div><div>La mère appelle <b>le garçon</b> pour le dîner.<br>The mother calls <b>the boy</b> for dinner.</div>','<div>The French word <b>le fils</b> translates to <b>the son</b> in English. It refers to a male offspring or a male child in relation to their parents.</div><div><b>Le fils</b> joue avec son chien.<br><b>The son</b> is playing with his dog.</div><div>Le père et <b>le fils</b> vont à la pêche.<br>The father and <b>the son</b> are going fishing.</div><div><b>Le fils</b> aide sa mère en cuisine.<br><b>The son</b> helps his mother in the kitchen.</div>','<div>The French word <b>le lion</b> translates to <b>the lion</b> in English. It refers to a large, carnivorous feline mammal, known for its strength and mane, and often referred to as the "king of the jungle" or "king of beasts".</div><div><b>Le lion</b> rugit dans la savane.<br><b>The lion</b> roars in the savannah.</div><div>Le zoo a accueilli un nouveau <b>lion</b>.<br>The zoo has welcomed a new <b>lion</b>.</div><div><b>Le lion</b> chasse principalement la nuit.<br><b>The lion</b> mainly hunts at night.</div>','<div>The French word <b>le boeuf</b> translates to <b>the ox</b> or <b>the beef</b> in English. It refers to the meat of a cow or bull, especially when prepared for eating. It can also refer to the animal itself in some contexts, particularly a castrated adult male cattle.</div><div>Le boucher vend du <b>boeuf</b> de qualité.<br><b>The butcher</b> sells high-quality beef.</div><div>Les agriculteurs élèvent du <b>boeuf</b> pour la viande.<br>Farmers raise <b>the oxen</b> for meat.</div><div>Je préfère le steak de <b>boeuf</b> au poulet.<br>I prefer <b>beef</b> steak to chicken.</div>','<div>The French word <b>la femme</b> translates to <b>the woman</b> in English. It refers to an adult human female.</div><div><b>La femme</b> lit un livre.<br><b>The woman</b> is reading a book.</div><div><b>La femme</b> traverse la rue.<br><b>The woman</b> is crossing the street.</div><div>Le chat de <b>la femme</b> est très doux.<br><b>The woman</b>’s cat is very soft.</div>','<div>The French word <b>la grand-mère</b> translates to <b>the grandmother</b> in English. It refers to the mother of one’s father or mother.</div><div><b>La grand-mère</b> raconte les meilleures histoires.<br><b>The grandmother</b> tells the best stories.</div><div><b>La grand-mère</b> prépare un gâteau d’anniversaire.<br><b>The grandmother</b> is baking a birthday cake.</div><div>Chaque dimanche, nous rendons visite à <b>la grand-mère</b>.<br>Every Sunday, we visit <b>the grandmother</b>.</div>','<div>The French phrase <b>la vache</b> translates to <b>the cow</b> in English when referring to the animal. However, it’s also used colloquially to express surprise, admiration, or sympathy, similar to exclaiming "Wow!" or "Holy cow!" in English.</div><div><b>La vache</b> broute dans le pré.<br><b>The cow</b> is grazing in the meadow.</div><div>Quand j’ai vu le prix, j’ai dit "<b>la vache</b>!"<br>When I saw the price, I said "<b>holy cow</b>!"</div><div>Elle s’est approchée de <b>la vache</b> pour la caresser.<br>She approached <b>the cow</b> to pet it.</div>','<div>The French word <b>la fleur</b> translates to <b>the flower</b> in English. It refers to the reproductive structure found in flowering plants, characterized by its beauty and fragrance.</div><div><b>La fleur</b> est le symbole de la beauté.<br><b>The flower</b> is a symbol of beauty.</div><div>J’ai acheté <b>la fleur</b> pour toi.<br>I bought <b>the flower</b> for you.</div><div><b>La fleur</b> du jardin embaume l’air.<br><b>The flower</b> in the garden perfumes the air.</div>','<div>The French word <b>l’homme</b> translates to <b>the man</b> in English. It refers to an adult male human being.</div><div><b>L’homme</b> marche dans la rue.<br><b>The man</b> is walking down the street.</div><div><b>L’homme</b> lit un livre.<br><b>The man</b> is reading a book.</div><div><b>L’homme</b> porte un manteau bleu.<br><b>The man</b> is wearing a blue coat.</div>','<div>The French word <b>l’oiseau</b> translates to <b>the bird</b> in English. It refers to any of the warm-blooded vertebrates constituting the class Aves, having feathers, wings, a beak, and typically being able to fly.</div><div><b>L’oiseau</b> s’envole du nid.<br><b>The bird</b> flies away from the nest.</div><div>Le chant de <b>l’oiseau</b> est mélodieux.<br>The song of <b>the bird</b> is melodious.</div><div><b>L’oiseau</b> picore des graines dans le jardin.<br><b>The bird</b> pecks at seeds in the garden.</div>','<div>The French word <b>l’éléphant</b> translates to <b>the elephant</b> in English. It refers to a large mammal known for its long trunk, large ears, and tusks, native to Africa and parts of Asia.</div><div><b>L’éléphant</b> se baigne dans le lac.<br><b>The elephant</b> bathes in the lake.</div><div>La trompe de <b>l’éléphant</b> est très puissante.<br>The trunk of <b>the elephant</b> is very powerful.</div><div><b>L’éléphant</b> mange des branches et des feuilles.<br><b>The elephant</b> eats branches and leaves.</div>','<div>The French word <b>l’arbre</b> translates to <b>the tree</b> in English. It refers to a perennial plant with an elongated stem, or trunk, supporting branches and leaves in most species.</div><div><b>L’arbre</b> ombrage le jardin.<br><b>The tree</b> shades the garden.</div><div>Les enfants grimpent à <b>l’arbre</b>.<br>The children climb <b>the tree</b>.</div><div><b>L’arbre</b> perd ses feuilles en automne.<br><b>The tree</b> loses its leaves in autumn.</div>','<div>The French phrase <b>les garçons</b> translates to <b>the boys</b> in English. It refers to a group of male children or young males.</div><div><b>Les garçons</b> jouent au football.<br><b>The boys</b> are playing soccer.</div><div><b>Les garçons</b> ont faim après l’école.<br><b>The boys</b> are hungry after school.</div><div><b>Les garçons</b> font leurs devoirs ensemble.<br><b>The boys</b> are doing their homework together.</div>','<div>The French word <b>les filles</b> translates to <b>the girls</b> in English. It refers to a group of female children or young women.</div><div><b>Les filles</b> jouent dans le jardin.<br><b>The girls</b> are playing in the garden.</div><div><b>Les filles</b> aiment lire des histoires.<br><b>The girls</b> like to read stories.</div><div>Chaque samedi, <b>les filles</b> vont à la danse.<br>Every Saturday, <b>the girls</b> go to dance class.</div>','<div>The French phrase <b>les poules</b> translates to <b>the chickens</b> in English. It refers to a group of domesticated birds commonly kept for their eggs or meat.</div><div><b>Les poules</b> pondent des oeufs.<br><b>The chickens</b> lay eggs.</div><div>Mon voisin élève <b>les poules</b> dans son jardin.<br>My neighbor raises <b>the chickens</b> in his garden.</div><div>J’aime écouter <b>les poules</b> caqueter le matin.<br>I like to listen to <b>the chickens</b> clucking in the morning.</div>','<div>The French word <b>les guitares</b> translates to <b>the guitars</b> in English. It is the plural form of "la guitare", referring to stringed musical instruments commonly used in many different genres of music.</div><div><b>Les guitares</b> sont accrochées au mur.<br><b>The guitars</b> are hung on the wall.</div><div>Il joue <b>la guitare</b> électrique et acoustique.<br>He plays both the electric and acoustic <b>guitar</b>.</div><div>Mon groupe a besoin de nouvelles <b>les guitares</b>.<br>My band needs new <b>guitars</b>.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>Le pont est large.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used here to specify the bridge being talked about.</div><div><strong>pont</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French meaning "bridge". It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject to its descriptor or state.</div><div><strong>large</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "wide" or "broad". In the sentence, it describes the bridge, matching the masculine singular form of the noun it modifies.</div>','<h2>Le chemin est étroit.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes a noun to specify the noun being referred to.</div><div><strong>chemin</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French, which means "path" or "way" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject to the adjective that describes it.</div><div><strong>étroit</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "narrow". It describes the subject noun, "chemin", in its masculine singular form to agree with the gender and number of the noun.</div>','<h2>Oui, je suis le garagiste.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It affirms a statement or responds positively to a question.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. Here, it links the subject "je" to the noun that follows, indicating a state of being or profession.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article for masculine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It specifies a particular individual or item.</div><div><strong>garagiste</strong> This is a French noun meaning "mechanic" or "garage owner". In this sentence, it is used as a profession, indicating what the person does.</div>','<h2>Le guépard est rapide.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used here to specify a particular example of a noun, in this case, "guepard".</div><div><strong>guépard</strong> This is a French noun meaning "cheetah". It denotes a specific type of animal known for its speed.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject of the sentence to the adjective that describes it.</div><div><strong>rapide</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "fast" or "quick". In this sentence, it describes the noun "guépard", indicating that the cheetah is a fast animal. It is in the masculine singular form to agree with the masculine singular noun "guépard".</div>','<h2>Nous avons gagné le jeu!</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>avons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this case, it is used as an auxiliary verb to form the passé composé, a past tense in French.</div><div><strong>gagné</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "gagner", which means "to win". Combined with the auxiliary verb "avons", it forms the passé composé, indicating a completed action in the past.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article in French for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>jeu</strong> This is a French noun meaning "game". It is masculine singular to match the definite article "le".</div>','<h2>C’est quoi le problème?</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is the contraction of "ce" and "est", the third-person singular of the verb "être", which means "to be". In English, this translates to "it is" or "that’s". It’s used here to point out or define something.</div><div><strong>quoi</strong> This is a French interrogative word meaning "what". It is used here to ask for information specifying something.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, used here to define "problème". In English, it translates to "the".</div><div><strong>problème</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French, meaning "problem" in English. It refers to any issue or matter that is under question or is in a state of dispute.</div>','<h2>As-tu vu le film?</h2><div><strong>As-tu</strong> This is a contraction of "As" and "tu" in French, which together form the present tense of the verb "avoir" (to have). "As" is the second-person singular form of "avoir", and "tu" is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. Here, it is used in a question to ask if the person "have" done something.</div><div><strong>vu</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "voir", which means "to see". When combined with "As-tu", it forms a perfect tense construction that translates as "have you seen".</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article "the" in English and is used before masculine singular nouns.</div><div><strong>film</strong> This is a French word for "movie" or "film". It is a masculine singular noun.</div>','<h2>Eh bien, voici le salon.</h2><div><strong>Eh bien</strong> This is a French expression used to acknowledge what has been said or to transition to a different subject. It can often be translated as "Well then" or "So" in English.</div><div><strong>voici</strong> This is a French demonstrative pronoun that means "here is" or "here are". It is used to present or introduce someone or something.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>salon</strong> This is a French noun meaning "living room" or "lounge". It refers to a room in a house or apartment where people can sit, relax, and socialize.</div>','<h2>Je vais prendre le pâté, aussi.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it’s used to express a future action, similar to "am going to" in English.</div><div><strong>prendre</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to take" in French, but when used after "aller", it means "to have" in the sense of "to order", as in ordering food at a restaurant.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article in French for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>pâté</strong> This is a French word meaning "pate", which is a spread made from meat, fish, or vegetables. It’s commonly used in French cuisine.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "also" or "too". It’s used here to add information, suggesting that the speaker will have the pâté in addition to something else.</div>','<h2>C’est quand le prochain examen?</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This phrase combines the pronoun "ce" (it/this) and the third-person singular of the verb "être" (to be), "est", forming the contraction "c’est", which translates to "it is" or "this is" in English. It’s often used to introduce a statement or a question.</div><div><strong>quand</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "when". It’s used to ask about the time or date of an event or situation.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article "the" in English, used here before a masculine singular noun. It denotes a specific thing or concept in the discussion.</div><div><strong>prochain</strong> This is an adjective meaning "next" in English. It describes something that follows immediately after in order, time, or space. Here, it agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is masculine singular.</div><div><strong>examen</strong> This is a masculine noun meaning "test" or "exam" in English. It refers to an evaluation or assessment usually given to students.</div>','<h2>Oui, je serai le conducteur.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It signals affirmation or agreement.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>serai</strong> This is the first-person singular future tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It indicates an action that will occur in the future, in this case, becoming the driver.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article in French, masculine singular form, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes a noun to define it as something specific and known to the speaker and listener.</div><div><strong>conducteur</strong> This is a French noun meaning "driver". It is masculine singular to match the article "le".</div>','<h2>Le film nous a fait peur.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used here to specify the film being talked about.</div><div><strong>film</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French, equivalent to "film" or "movie" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>nous</strong> This is a first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "us" in English. It’s functioning as the direct object of the verb phrase "a fait peur" in this sentence, indicating who was scared by the movie.</div><div><strong>a fait</strong> This is the third-person singular perfect tense form of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make" in English. Here, it is conjugated with the auxiliary verb "avoir" (to have) and is used to describe a completed action that affected the participants. The phrase "a fait" means "has made" or "has caused".</div><div><strong>peur</strong> This is a French noun meaning "fear" or "scare". In this sentence, it’s used as the object of "a fait" to express what the film has caused to the subject.</div>','<h2>Le vieux camion est terne.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It refers specifically to one item that is already known by the speakers or that has been previously mentioned.</div><div><strong>vieux</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "old". It is used here in the masculine singular form to match the noun it modifies. In this context, it describes the age or state of the truck.</div><div><strong>camion</strong> This is a French noun that means "truck". It is masculine singular and is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It correlates the subject with the adjective following it, stating what the subject is or is like.</div><div><strong>terne</strong> This French adjective means "dull" or "lackluster". It is used here in the masculine singular form to describe the truck’s appearance.</div>','<h2>Est-ce que le service est compris?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce que</strong> This is a French phrase used to introduce a yes-no question, equivalent to "Is it that" in English. It’s a common way to form questions in French.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article for masculine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>service</strong> This is a French noun meaning "service". In this context, it could refer to the service charge or tip in a restaurant or establishment.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It links the subject to the attribute or state of being.</div><div><strong>compris</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "comprendre", meaning "to understand" or "to include" in English. In this context, it means "included". When used with "est", the phrase "est compris" translates as "is included".</div>','<h2>Tu es derrière le camion.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state of being or position of the subject in relation to something else.</div><div><strong>derrière</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "behind". It indicates the position of the subject in relation to the object mentioned next.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>camion</strong> This is a French noun meaning "truck". It is masculine and singular in this context, following the definite article.</div>','<h2>On va gagner le match.</h2><div><strong>On</strong> This is an indefinite pronoun, often used in French in place of "nous" (we) to mean "one" or "we". In this sentence, it functions as the subject and generally means "we".</div><div><strong>va</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". Despite "on" being used for "we", the verb form is third-person singular, not plural. In this context, "va" is used to form the future tense, translating to "is going" as in "we are going".</div><div><strong>gagner</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to win". In English, infinitive verbs are generally preceded by "to". Here, "gagner" follows the verb "aller", forming a future tense construction that translates to "going to win".</div><div><strong>le match</strong> "Le" is the masculine singular definite article "the", and "match" is a noun that means "game" or "match". In this phrase, "le" agrees in gender and number with the masculine noun "match", together meaning "the match".</div>','<h2>Je déteste laver le plancher.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>déteste</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "détester", which means "to hate". This verb expresses a strong dislike or hatred towards something.</div><div><strong>laver</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "laver", which means "to wash" or "to clean". In this sentence, it is used to indicate the action that the subject hates to do.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article "the" in English. It’s used here to define a specific object.</div><div><strong>plancher</strong> This is a French noun meaning "floor". In the context of this sentence, it refers to the specific thing that the speaker hates to clean.</div>','<h2>Je vais souvent marcher dans le parc.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it’s part of a structure (vais + infinitive) indicating a future or planned action.</div><div><strong>souvent</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "often". It indicates the frequency of the action.</div><div><strong>marcher</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "marcher", which means "to walk". Used after the verb "vais" it describes the action the subject intends to perform.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "in" or "into". It introduces the location where the action will take place.</div><div><strong>le parc</strong> This phrase consists of the definite article "le" (meaning "the") and the noun "parc" (meaning "park"). Together, they refer to a specific park where the subject is going to walk.</div>','<h2>Le plombier a pu réparer mon évier.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is the definite article in French for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>plombier</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French, which means "plumber". It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to the person who performs plumbing tasks.</div><div><strong>a pu</strong> This is the past compound of the verb "pouvoir" in the third-person singular. "A" is the auxiliary verb "avoir" in the present tense, and "pu" is the past participle of "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". Together, "a pu" translates to "was able to" or "could".</div><div><strong>réparer</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French, meaning "to repair" or "to fix". It’s used here to indicate the action performed by the subject (the plumber).</div><div><strong>mon</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, masculine singular form, meaning "my". It indicates that the noun it modifies belongs to the speaker.</div><div><strong>évier</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French, which means "sink". It’s the direct object of the verb "réparer", indicating what was repaired.</div>','<h2>Cette voiture est la meilleure.</h2><div><strong>Cette</strong> This is the French demonstrative adjective meaning "this" or "that" in English. It modifies the noun to point out a specific thing and must agree in gender (feminine) and number (singular) with the noun it describes, which is "car" in this case.</div><div><strong>voiture</strong> This is a French noun meaning "car". It is feminine and singular to match with the demonstrative adjective "cette".</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject "cette voiture" to its descriptive adjective.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the French definite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>meilleure</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the superlative adjective "meilleur" which means "best". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies. Here, it’s complementing "car" by stating it is the "best" among its kind.</div>','<h2>La nourriture était très bonne.</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the French definite article used for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It specifies that the speaker is talking about a particular food.</div><div><strong>nourriture</strong> This is a French noun meaning "food". It’s feminine and singular in this context, following the definite article "la".</div><div><strong>était</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes a past state of being or condition for the subject "la nourriture".</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is an adverb meaning "very" in English. It’s used to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>bonne</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "good". In this sentence, it’s in the feminine singular form to match "la nourriture". If the noun were masculine, it would be "bon".</div>','<h2>Une personne parmi la foule</h2><div><strong>Une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for the feminine singular, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It precedes a feminine singular noun.</div><div><strong>personne</strong> This is a French noun meaning "person". It is feminine and singular, matching with the indefinite article "une".</div><div><strong>parmi</strong> This is a French preposition that means "among" or "amidst". It’s used to indicate the presence of someone or something within a group or a crowd.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the French definite article for the feminine singular, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes a feminine singular noun.</div><div><strong>foule</strong> This is a French noun meaning "crowd". It is feminine and singular, matching with the definite article "la".</div>','<h2>La neige tombe sur le sol.</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the French definite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It’s used here to specify the snow that is falling.</div><div><strong>neige</strong> This is a French noun meaning "snow". It’s feminine and singular to match the definite article "la".</div><div><strong>tombe</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "tomber", which means "to fall". It describes the action taking place, performed by the subject "la neige".</div><div><strong>sur</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "on" or "upon". It shows the relationship between the snow and the ground, indicating where the snow is falling.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It’s used here to specify the ground that the snow is falling upon.</div><div><strong>sol</strong> This is a French noun meaning "ground" or "floor". It’s masculine and singular to match the definite article "le".</div>','<h2>Nous sourions pour la photo.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the French first-person plural pronoun equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sourions</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "sourire", which means "to smile". It describes the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a French preposition that means "for" in English. It is used to explain the purpose or reason for an action.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the French definite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun "photo".</div><div><strong>photo</strong> This is the French word for "photo" or "photograph". Here, it’s a feminine singular noun that is the object of the preposition "pour".</div>','<h2>Peux-tu allumer la lumière?</h2><div><strong>Peux-tu</strong> This is a contraction of "peux" (from "pouvoir" which means "can" or "to be able to") and "tu" (the second-person singular pronoun "you"). In this form, it’s used to ask if you can do something, similar to "Can you" in English.</div><div><strong>allumer</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "allumer", which means "to turn on" or "to light up". It’s used here to ask someone to turn on the light.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article "the" for feminine singular nouns in French. It precedes "lumière" which is feminine.</div><div><strong>lumière?</strong> This is a French noun meaning "light". In this sentence, it is the object being turned on.</div>','<h2>Il descend la côte à vélo.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>descend</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "descendre", which means "to go down" or "to descend". It describes the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>la côte</strong> This phrase means "the hill" or "the slope" in English. "La" is the feminine definite article "the", and "côte" is a feminine noun meaning "slope" or "hill".</div><div><strong>à vélo</strong> This phrase means "by bike" or "on a bike" in English. "À" is a preposition that can mean "to", "at", or "by", and "vélo" is a masculine noun that means "bike".</div>','<h2>Il essaie de voler la voiture.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>essaie</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "essayer", which means "to try". He is attempting to do something.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This word is a preposition often used to indicate the infinitive form of a verb that follows. In this case, it’s part of the verbal phrase "de voler" ("to steal").</div><div><strong>voler</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb which means "to steal". In the structure of this sentence, it shows the action he is trying to accomplish.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article for feminine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>voiture</strong> This is a feminine singular noun meaning "car". It’s the object of the sentence, the thing that is being tried to be stolen.</div>','<h2>La télévision est incluse, monsieur.</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the feminine singular definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes a feminine noun.</div><div><strong>télévision</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French, which means "television". It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject of the sentence to its complement.</div><div><strong>incluse</strong> This is the past participle of the French verb "inclure", which means "to include". It agrees in gender and number with the subject it refers to, in this case, "télévision", and is used here as an adjective.</div><div><strong>monsieur</strong> This is a French term of address for a man, equivalent to "sir" or "mister" in English. It directly addresses the person being spoken to.</div>','<h2>La soupe aux légumes est délicieuse.</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the French definite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes a feminine noun.</div><div><strong>soupe</strong> This is a French feminine noun meaning "soup". In this sentence, it’s the subject.</div><div><strong>aux</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "à" (meaning "to" or "with") and the definite article "les" (meaning "the"). When combined as "aux", it typically means "with" in the sense of ingredient inclusion. This preposition is used to describe something that contains or is associated with what follows it.</div><div><strong>légumes</strong> This is the French word for "vegetables". It’s a plural noun often used after "aux" to indicate the main ingredients in the soup.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject of the sentence to its complement or describes the subject’s state of being.</div><div><strong>délicieuse</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "delicious". Here, it describes the soup. The adjective agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes, in this case, feminine singular to match with "soupe".</div>','<h2>Est-ce que tu répares la machine?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce que</strong> It is a French phrase used to introduce a question. It doesn’t literally translate into English but would correspond to the inversion of subject and verb when asking a question (e.g., "Do" you repair the machine?). It can be seen as a question marker.</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person in an informal way.</div><div><strong>répares</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "réparer", which means "to repair". It denotes the action being applied to the object by the subject.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the feminine singular definite article in French, meaning "the" in English.</div><div><strong>machine</strong> This is a French noun meaning "machine" in English. It is a feminine noun, thus it has the article "la" preceding it.</div>','<h2>La feuille de papier est mince.</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the French definite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes a specific item that is known to the speaker and listener.</div><div><strong>feuille</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French, which means "sheet" as in a sheet of paper. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a French preposition which typically means "of" in English. In this context, it is used to indicate the material or content of the noun it precedes.</div><div><strong>papier</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French, which means "paper". Paired with "de", it specifies what the "feuille" is made of, thus "sheet of paper".</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject of the sentence, "la feuille de papier", to its descriptor.</div><div><strong>mince</strong> This is an adjective in French which means "thin". It describes the quality of the "feuille de papier" and agrees in number and gender with the feminine singular subject "la feuille".</div>','<h2>Vous souriez pour la photo.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It can indicate a formal address or refer to more than one person and is used here as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>souriez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural present tense form of the verb "sourire", which means "to smile". It indicates the action being performed by the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "for". It’s used to indicate the purpose of the action or whom the action is directed towards.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article in French, meaning "the". It is feminine singular to agree with the noun it precedes.</div><div><strong>photo</strong> This is a French noun meaning "picture" or "photo". It is feminine singular and is the object of the sentence—the thing for which the subject is smiling.</div>','<h2>Tu aurais aimé voir la mer.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is the subject of the sentence, referring to the person being spoken to in an informal manner.</div><div><strong>aurais</strong> This is the conditional tense of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this case, it is used to express a condition or hypothesis, "would have" in English.</div><div><strong>aimé</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love". Used with "aurais", it forms the conditional perfect, expressing a liking or wishing for something in the past.</div><div><strong>voir</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to see" in French. Here, it is used in conjunction with the auxiliary verb and the past participle to indicate the action that the subject would have liked to do.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article for feminine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>mer</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French meaning "sea".</div>','<h2>Nous nous amusons dans la neige.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, used here as a reflexive pronoun.</div><div><strong>nous</strong> This is the reflexive pronoun used with the reflexive verb "amuser" (to have fun). It relates the action back to the subject.</div><div><strong>amusons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the reflexive verb "s’amuser", which means "to have fun". It describes the action the subject is taking.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition meaning "in" or "inside" in English. It’s used to indicate location.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article "the" in English, used here for feminine singular nouns.</div><div><strong>neige</strong> This is a French noun meaning "snow". It’s a feminine noun which is why it is preceded by "la".</div>','<h2>Oui, j’étais à l’intérieur de la banque.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It is used to affirm a statement or respond affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>j’</strong> This is a contraction of "je", which is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. The contraction is used before a vowel sound for ease of pronunciation.</div><div><strong>étais</strong> This is the first-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It is used to describe a past state of being or condition.</div><div><strong>à l’intérieur</strong> This phrase means "inside" in English. It’s a prepositional phrase composed of "à" (to) and "l’intérieur" (the interior).</div><div><strong>de</strong> This word is a preposition which can mean "of" or "from" in English. It is used here to link the noun "la banque" with the previous phrase, indicating location.</div><div><strong>la banque</strong> This is a feminine noun meaning "the bank" in English. "La" is the feminine singular definite article, and "banque" is the noun it modifies.</div>','<h2>La voiture ne fonctionnait pas normalement.</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the definite article in French for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun "voiture" (car).</div><div><strong>voiture</strong> This is a feminine noun in French that means "car". It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is a part of the negation in French. When paired with "pas", it negates the verb that follows. In written French, "ne" precedes the verb.</div><div><strong>fonctionnait</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "fonctionner", which means "to function" or "to work". It indicates an ongoing action in the past.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negation in French. Together with "ne", it negates the verb "fonctionnait". It is placed after the verb in written French.</div><div><strong>normalement</strong> This is an adverb in French meaning "normally". It modifies the verb "fonctionnait" to indicate that the car was not functioning as it normally would.</div>','<h2>Nous montions la montagne en télésiège.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a group including the speaker.</div><div><strong>montions</strong> This is the first-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "monter", which means "to climb" or "to go up". It describes a continuous past action or a habitual action in the past.</div><div><strong>la montagne</strong> This phrase means "the mountain" in English. "La" is the definite article for feminine singular nouns, and "montagne" is a feminine singular noun meaning mountain.</div><div><strong>en</strong> This is a French preposition that can have several meanings. In this sentence, it is used to indicate the means by which the action happens, similar to "by" or "on" in English.</div><div><strong>télésiège</strong> This is a French masculine noun meaning "chairlift" in English. It refers to the type of lift used to ascend mountains, especially in ski resorts.</div>','<h2>J’ai réparé l’ordinateur.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contraction of "je", the first-person singular pronoun in French, which means "I" in English. It’s contracted to "j’" because the following verb starts with a vowel, for ease of pronunciation.</div><div><strong>ai</strong> This is the first-person singular of the auxiliary verb "avoir" (to have) in the present tense. In compound tenses, "avoir" is used to form the past participle.</div><div><strong>réparé</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "réparer", which means "to repair" in English. In this sentence, it is used in conjunction with the verb "avoir" to form the passé composé, which indicates the action of repairing is completed.</div><div><strong>l’ordinateur</strong> This is a combination of "le" (the) and "ordinateur", where "le" contracts to "l’" before a vowel or mute h. "Ordinateur" means "computer" in English. "L’ordinateur" is the direct object of the sentence, receiving the action of the verb "réparé".</div>','<h2>Merci pour l’invitation.</h2><div><strong>Merci</strong> This is the French word for "thank you". It’s an expression of gratitude.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This preposition means "for" in English. It’s used here to indicate the reason for the gratitude.</div><div><strong>l’</strong> This is the contracted form of "le" or "la" in front of a vowel or mute "h", which are the French definite articles for "the". In this case, "l’" stands in place of "la" because "invitation" begins with a vowel sound and is feminine. The contraction is mandatory for pronunciation ease.</div><div><strong>invitation</strong> This is a French noun meaning "invitation". It refers to the action of inviting someone or the card/document used to invite someone. In this context, it is the object that the speaker is thankful for.</div>','<h2>L’autoroute est droite.</h2><div><strong>L’autoroute</strong> This is the French word for "the highway". It’s a feminine singular noun referring to a major road designed for fast traffic.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It indicates a state of being or existence.</div><div><strong>droite</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "straight". In this sentence, it describes the state or condition of "l’autoroute". As "autoroute" is feminine singular, the adjective "droite" also takes the feminine singular form to agree with the noun it describes.</div>','<h2>Nous avons couru dans l’océan.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>avons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it is used to form the compound past tense, known as the passé composé.</div><div><strong>couru</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "courir", which means "to run". Together with the auxiliary verb "avons", it forms the passé composé, expressing a completed action in the past.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French that means "in" or "into" when indicating direction or location.</div><div><strong>l’océan</strong> This is a masculine singular noun meaning "the ocean". The definite article "l’" is a contraction of "le" in front of a vowel or silent "h" and is used here to denote a specific ocean.</div>','<h2>Où est-ce que je peux payer l’addition?</h2><div><strong>Où</strong> This is the French interrogative adverb meaning "where". It is used to ask about location.</div><div><strong>est-ce que</strong> This is a French phrase that is used to introduce a question. It literally translates to "is it that" and is a means to turn a statement into a question.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>peux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It indicates the speaker’s ability or possibility to perform an action.</div><div><strong>payer</strong> This is the infinitive form of the French verb meaning "to pay". In this sentence, it is used in conjunction with "peux" to form the phrase "can pay".</div><div><strong>l’addition</strong> This is a French noun meaning "the bill" or "the check" in a restaurant context. "L’" is the contraction of "le" or "la" (the definite article in French) with the following word "addition" because "addition" starts with a vowel.</div>','<h2>Nous sommes entrés par l’arrière.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sommes</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It serves as an auxiliary verb in this sentence to form the passé composé (compound past tense).</div><div><strong>entrés</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "entrer", which means "to enter". Used with the auxiliary verb "sommes", it forms the passé composé to describe a completed action in the past. "Entrés" is the masculine plural form to agree with the subject "nous".</div><div><strong>par</strong> This is a preposition that can be translated to "by" or "through" in English. It indicates the means or route taken to achieve the action.</div><div><strong>l’arrière</strong> This is a noun phrase that means "the rear" or "the back" in English. The article "l’" is a contraction of "le" before a vowel or mute "h", and "arrière" is the noun it refers to. It’s used here to indicate the location or point of entry.</div>','<h2>Près de l’entrée, à votre droite.</h2><div><strong>Près de</strong> This is a French prepositional phrase meaning "near" or "close to". It indicates proximity to a location or object.</div><div><strong>l’entrée</strong> This is the French word for "the entrance". It’s a feminine singular noun and the contraction of "la entrée" due to the vowel sound at the beginning of "entrée".</div><div><strong>à</strong> This French preposition can have several meanings including "to", "at", and "in", but in this context, it helps indicate a specific position or direction relative to something, here translated as "at" or "on".</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is the French possessive adjective meaning "your". It is used here in the singular form to address the person respectfully or to refer to something that belongs to a person you are conversing with.</div><div><strong>droite</strong> This is the French word for "right" as in the right side. It is often used with the preposition "à" in the context of giving directions, to indicate the right-hand side or direction.</div>','<h2>L’orage est pire que prévu.</h2><div><strong>L’</strong> This is the contracted form of "le" (the) before a word starting with a vowel or mute h, and in this case, it’s attached to the word "orage". It denotes that "orage" is masculine singular.</div><div><strong>orage</strong> This is the French word for "storm". It is a masculine singular noun, which refers to a violent disturbance of the atmosphere with strong winds, usually accompanied by rain, thunder, lightning, or snow.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It is used here as a linking verb to connect the subject "l’orage" to its description of being worse than expected.</div><div><strong>pire</strong> This is the French comparative adjective meaning "worse". It is used to indicate that something is of lower quality or more undesirable in comparison to something else.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a French conjunction or relative pronoun that means "than" when used in comparisons. It introduces the standard against which the comparison is made.</div><div><strong>prévu</strong> This is the past participle of the French verb "prévoir", meaning "to forecast" or "to predict". When used as an adjective like here, it means "expected" or "planned". In this sentence, it’s used to show the discrepancy between the expected intensity of the storm and its actual intensity.</div>','<h2>Mon chien aime courir dans l’eau.</h2><div><strong>Mon</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French that translates to "my" in English. It indicates that the noun it modifies belongs to the speaker. It’s in the masculine singular form to agree with the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>chien</strong> This is a French noun meaning "dog". It’s in the masculine singular form as indicated by the possessive adjective "mon".</div><div><strong>aime</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to love" or "to like". Here, it indicates that the subject enjoys the action described afterwards.</div><div><strong>courir</strong> This is the infinitive form of a French verb meaning "to run". It’s used here to describe the action the dog enjoys doing.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "in" or "into". It indicates the location where the action of the verb is taking place.</div><div><strong>l’eau</strong> This is a combination of the definite article "l’" which means "the" and the noun "eau" which means "water". The article contracts to "l’" because "eau" begins with a vowel. This noun phrase translates to "the water" in English.</div>','<h2>Tu as dû attendre l’avion pendant deux heures.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>as</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", which means "to have". Here, it is used to form the past tense, known as the passé composé.</div><div><strong>dû</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must". In this sentence, it indicates an obligation that was in the past.</div><div><strong>attendre</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to wait". In this sentence, it is linked with the auxiliary verb and past participle to mean "had to wait".</div><div><strong>l’avion</strong> This is a phrase that consists of the definite article "le" contracted with the vowel starting noun "avion" to form "l’avion", meaning "the airplane".</div><div><strong>pendant</strong> This is a preposition that means "during" in English. It’s used to indicate the duration of time over which an action takes place.</div><div><strong>deux heures</strong> This phrase means "two hours". "Deux" is the number two and "heures" is the plural form of "heure", which means "hour". Together, they specify the length of time that was spent waiting.</div>','<h2>Il est parmi les meilleurs.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state of being of the subject.</div><div><strong>parmi</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "among" or "amongst". It indicates the subject’s position within a group.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It is plural because it refers to a group of people or things.</div><div><strong>meilleurs</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "best". It’s in the masculine plural form to agree with the noun it describes. If it were referring to a feminine group, it would be "meilleures".</div>','<h2>J’aime sentir les fleurs.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is a contraction of "je", the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. The contraction is used before a vowel or mute "h" for ease of pronunciation.</div><div><strong>aime</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to love" or "to like". It indicates what the subject feels or enjoys.</div><div><strong>sentir</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "sentir", which means "to smell" or "to feel". In this sentence, it functions as the main verb following the verb "aime" to describe what the subject loves to do.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article for plural nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It indicates specific flowers are being talked about.</div><div><strong>fleurs</strong> This is the French word for "flowers", a plural noun referring to more than one flower. It’s the object of the verb "sentir".</div>','<h2>Quels seront les résultats?</h2><div><strong>Quels</strong> This is the plural interrogative adjective in French, which means "which" or "what" in English. It is used here to ask about multiple items/results, and it agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is "résultats" (results).</div><div><strong>seront</strong> This is the third-person plural future tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It indicates what the results "will be" in the future.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article for plural nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It specifies the noun "résultats", suggesting specific results are being asked about.</div><div><strong>résultats</strong> This is a plural noun meaning "results" or "outcomes" in English. The entire question is inquiring what these results or outcomes will be.</div>','<h2>Quels sont les ingrédients?</h2><div><strong>Quels</strong> This is the French word for "which" or "what". It’s the plural masculine form of the interrogative adjective used to ask for specific information about a noun (in this case, "les ingrédients").</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It is used to link the subject to the predicate in the sentence.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It’s used here in the plural form to refer to a specific set of ingredients.</div><div><strong>ingrédients</strong> This is the French word for "ingredients". It’s a noun in plural form, indicating the components of a recipe or parts that compose something.</div>','<h2>Les biscuits sentent très bon.</h2><div><strong>Les</strong> This is the French definite article used for plural nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It indicates that the biscuits being referred to are specific and known to both the speaker and the listener.</div><div><strong>biscuits</strong> This is a plural noun in French which means "cookies" or "biscuits" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sentent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "sentir", which means "to smell". It describes the action being carried out by the subject (the biscuits).</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is an adverb meaning "very" in English. It’s used to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>bon</strong> This is an adjective meaning "good" in English. It’s in the masculine singular form, but since adjectives in French agree in number with the noun they describe and "les biscuits" is plural, the meaning understood is "good" in a plural sense (though the adjective form doesn’t change).</div>','<h2>Je nage avec les dauphins.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>nage</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "nager", which means "to swim". It describes the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "with". It is used here to indicate the company in which the action is being performed.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used to specify the noun that follows it.</div><div><strong>dauphins</strong> This is a French noun meaning "dolphins". It is plural to denote more than one dolphin and is the object of the preposition "avec".</div>','<h2>Tu dois arroser les plantes.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person in an informal context. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>dois</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the modal verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must" in English. It indicates an obligation or a necessity relating to the subject "tu".</div><div><strong>arroser</strong> This is the infinitive form of a French verb which means "to water" or "to sprinkle" in English. It is used here to indicate the action that needs to be completed by the subject.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the French definite article for plural nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It’s used here to specify the exact plants that need to be watered.</div><div><strong>plantes</strong> This is the plural form of the French word "plante", which means "plants". It refers to the items that need to be watered.</div>','<h2>Je suis allé dans les Alpes pour skier.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". However, in this context, it’s combined with "allé" as part of a compound verb in the passé composé tense to express the past action of going.</div><div><strong>allé</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "aller", which means "to go". When combined with "suis" it denotes that the action has been completed in the past.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition that means "in" or "to" when talking about entering a place. It describes where the subject went.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French for plural nouns, here translating to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>Alpes</strong> This is a proper noun referring to the Alps, a large mountain range in Europe. In French, proper nouns are capitalized like in English.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition that means "for" in English. It is used to indicate the purpose of the action.</div><div><strong>skier</strong> This is the infinitive verb "to ski" in French, expressing the purpose of the subject’s trip to the Alps.</div>','<h2>Tu aimeras les glissades d’eau.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>aimeras</strong> This is the second-person singular future tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to love" or "to like". It indicates an action that will take place in the future.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It’s used here to specify the noun that follows.</div><div><strong>glissades</strong> This is the French word for "slides". It is a noun in plural form.</div><div><strong>d’eau</strong> This is a French compound noun, made up of the word "eau" which means "water", and the preposition "de" which denotes possession or belonging, i.e., "slides of water" or "water slides".</div>','<h2>Il m’a expliqué les changements.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>m’a</strong> This is a contraction of "me" (object pronoun "me") and "a" (third-person singular past tense of "avoir", used as the auxiliary verb in compound tenses). It means "has to me" or, more naturally in English, "to me has". This contraction indicates that the speaker is the recipient of the explanation.</div><div><strong>expliqué</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "expliquer", which means "to explain". In this sentence, it is used with the auxiliary verb "a" to form the past tense, known as the passé composé.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the plural definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the plural noun "changements" to specify these particular changes.</div><div><strong>changements</strong> This is the plural form of the French noun "changement", which means "changes". In the sentence, it is the direct object that receives the action of the verb "explained".</div>','<h2>Je lui donnerai bientôt les clés.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence and indicates who will perform the action.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun in French that can mean "him" or "her" in English, depending on the context. In this sentence, it indicates the person to whom the keys will be given.</div><div><strong>donnerai</strong> This is the first-person singular future tense form of the verb "donner", which means "to give" in English. It indicates that the action of giving will occur in the future.</div><div><strong>bientôt</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "soon" in English. It gives information about the timing of the action.</div><div><strong>les clés</strong> This phrase means "the keys" in English. "Les" is the definite article for plural nouns in French, and "clés" is the plural noun for "keys".</div>','<h2>Mon résultat n’est pas dans les choix.</h2><div><strong>Mon</strong> This is the possessive adjective for the first-person singular in French, equivalent to "my" in English. It indicates ownership or association with the noun that follows.</div><div><strong>résultat</strong> This is a French noun that means "result" or "outcome". It’s masculine and singular to match the possessive adjective "mon".</div><div><strong>n’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ne" and "est", which is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être" (to be). This contraction means "is not" and is part of a negation structure in French.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is part of the negation in French, which translates to "not" in English. Together with "ne/n’", it negates the verb of the sentence.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French, meaning "in" or "inside". It indicates the location or presence within something.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This French definite article is plural for "the". It precedes the noun to indicate a specific set of items. "les" is the plural form of "le" or "la".</div><div><strong>choix</strong> This is a French noun that means "choices" or "options". It is masculine plural to agree with the definite article "les".</div>','<h2>Tu vois ce que les joueurs tiennent?</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person in an informal context. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vois</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "voir", which means "to see". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>ce que</strong> This is a combination of the demonstrative pronoun "ce" (which means "this") and the relative pronoun "que" (which means "that" or "which"). Together, "ce que" introduces a dependent clause and means "what" in English.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French for masculine plural nouns, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>joueurs</strong> This is the French word for "players". It is a masculine plural noun. In this sentence, it is the object of the action in the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>tiennent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "tenir", which means "to hold". It describes the action being performed by the players.</div>','<h2>Vous surveillerez les enfants.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. In this sentence, it is used as the subject and implies either a formal address or addresses more than one person.</div><div><strong>surveillerez</strong> This is the future tense of the verb "surveiller", which means "to watch over" or "to supervise". It is conjugated with the subject "vous", indicating that you (either singular formal or plural) will be doing the action of supervising.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used here to specify the particular children being talked about.</div><div><strong>enfants</strong> This is a French noun meaning "children". It is in the plural form and is the direct object of the sentence, receiving the action of being watched over.</div>','<h2> Oui. Quels sont les horaires de travail?</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It’s used to affirmatively respond to a question or statement.</div><div><strong>Quels</strong> This is the plural form of the interrogative adjective "quel", which means "which" or "what" in English. It’s used here to inquire about something, specifically about "horaires de travail" (work schedules).</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. In this question, it links the subject "quels" with the complement that describes it (the work schedules).</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article for plural nouns in French, translating as "the" in English. It’s used here to refer to a specific set of schedules (the work schedules).</div><div><strong>horaires de travail</strong> This is a noun phrase where "horaires" means "schedules" and "de travail" means "of work". Together, "horaires de travail" translates as "work schedules". The phrase is the object of the inquiry about what the work schedules are.</div>','<h2>Les montgolfières voleront bientôt.</h2><div><strong>Les</strong> This is the French definite article for plural nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the plural noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>montgolfières</strong> This is a French noun meaning "hot air balloons". It’s a plural noun referring to more than one balloon.</div><div><strong>voleront</strong> This is a verb in future tense, third-person plural form of the verb "voler", which means "to fly". It indicates what the hot air balloons will do in the future.</div><div><strong>bientôt</strong> This is an adverb meaning "soon". It tells us when the hot air balloons will fly.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);